  rolled back manually or continued. However, the -bail option will cause the
  shell to exit immediately upon the error in the DELETE statement.

  But the command includes COMMIT after the DELETE. Because of -bail, the shell
  will exit when the DELETE fails, so the COMMIT will never be executed.
  Therefore, the transaction will be rolled back automatically? Actually, in
  SQLite, if we have an active transaction and the program exits without
  committing, the transaction is rolled back.

  So the sequence of events:

  1. PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON; -> success
  2. BEGIN; -> starts a transaction
  3. DELETE FROM customers WHERE id = 7; -> This statement fails because of the
  foreign key constraint (RESTRICT). So the row is not deleted. But because of
  -bail, the shell exits immediately with an error without running COMMIT.

  Therefore, the entire transaction is rolled back. The DELETE operation does
  not take place.

